Hi Brian - in my experience (both as author and reviewer), the quality of the
plots will not dictate whether an article is publishable or not.  However, it
is likely that you would be asked - if the article is accepted - to provide a
version of the graphics without the grey background.  Chapter 8 of Hadley's
book "Polishing your plots for publication" deals with this very issue.  The
default theme shows the background as grey however this can be changed by
setting the theme to black & white - look-up the "theme_update" option and see
example of using theme_set(theme_bw()).
Best wishes,
Alan

_______________________
Dr. Alan Kelly
Trinity College Dublin

   Quoting Brian Weaver <the...@iastate.edu>:

> ggploters,
>
> this is not a technical question involving ggplot2 but more of an experience
> question.  I am currently in the process of trying to publish two different
> articles and my other coauthors are very nervous about publishing with
> plots created with ggplot2, mainly because the background is gray.  They are
> concerned that journals will reject the articles because the plots do not
> have the white background.  My guess is their reasoning for this concern is
> journals are of course concerned about saving money and adding additional
> "color" to the background of the plot will add to the expense of
> publication.  Have any of you submitted articles to journals that had plots
> from ggplot2.  Also, if you have tried, were any of them rejected or
> criticized for the plots.
>
> I appreciate your comments and look forward to hearing about your
> experiences.
>
> Brian
