Rob,

What you could do to get around the problem with the 180 degrees, is to subtract 360 from all the positive longitudinal values (in case you're just covering the Pacific, that should get rid of the arbitrary cutoff, since  -180 = +180)

Then your first strategy would do:

xrng <- c(-185,-110)
idx <- which(test$ssm_lon > 0)
test$ssm_lon[idx] <- test$ssm_lon[idx]-360

m <- ggplot(data=test, aes(ssm_lon, ssm_lat,group=seal))+

geom_path(aes(colour=factor(seal)))+

borders('world')+
coord_cartesian(xlim=xrng, ylim=yrng)
print(m)

Heike

