Hi James,
In short it is not easy to do what you want with ggplot (assuming I
understand what you want in the first place...). The histogram and the
timeseries plots would not share either axis, so faceting really does
not work well here. You can do something like

dat.ts <- as.data.frame(t(mult.ts))
dat.ts.m <- melt(dat.ts)
dat.ts.m$time <- 1:100
dat.ts.m$Group <- "Time series"

dat.scores <- data.frame(variable=NA, value=NA, time=ts.scores, Group
= "Histogram", stringsAsFactors=FALSE)

dat.final <- rbind(dat.ts.m, dat.scores)
ggplot(dat.final) + geom_histogram(aes(x=time, y=..count.., group=1),
binwidth=.75, data=dat.scores) + geom_line(aes(x=time, y=value,
group=variable), data=dat.ts.m) + facet_wrap(~Group, scales="free")

But this is wrong in many ways, most importantly that the x-axis label
is wrong for the histogram and the y label is wrong from the time
series.

Heres hoping somebody proves me wrong, but I think it will be quite
difficult with ggplot2 to produce a workable graph such as you
described.

-Ista

