  I don't think it's so much a data-organization issue as an issue with
philosophy/intent of ggplot faceting.  As I understand it, faceting is
designed to facilitate creation of 'small multiples' -- i.e., a series
of plots that have similar or identical formatting but represent
different slices or subsets of the data. What you want is **two
different views of the same data**.  This is reasonable, it's just not
the purpose (I think) of faceting. Tools like grid.arrange() in the
gridExtra package should make this sort of thing easier ... (sorry, no
example right now).

