MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

If all you want is a pie chart, why not use base?

# I renamed your data object because data is also a function name
dat <- data.frame(cluster = paste("Cluster", 1:4), size = c(0.5519,
0.2238, 0.1544, 0.0698))

# pie chart with labels
# (side puzzle, which one of these colours is out of place?)
with(dat, pie(x = size, labels = paste(cluster, size, sep = ": "),
              col = c("tomato3", "honeydew3", "papayawhip", "mintcream")))

# 4, 'mintcream'. Tomatoes, honeydew melons, and papayas are all
fruits, mintcream is not.

Cheers,

Josh

On Thu, Sep 30, 2010 at 9:16 AM, Data Analytics Corp.
<wa...@dataanalyticscorp.com> wrote:
> If I have to use SigmaPlot, Excel or Inkscape, then what's the purpose of
> ggplot2????

Consider these plots:

x <-c(.22, .25, .28, .25)
pie(x)
plot(x=1:4, y = x, ylim = c(0, .4))

x2 <- sample(x, 8, replace = TRUE)
pie(x2)
plot(x = 1:8, y = x2, ylim = c(0, .4))

These are not beautiful examples, but at least for me, it is much
slower and harder to read the pie chart than the scatter plot.
ggplot2 will easily do the scatter plot (and make it look much nicer
than the crass thing I presented here).

